== Chapter 1

#align(center)[THE KNOWLEDGE OF GOD AND OF OURSELVES MUTUALLY CONNECTED.—NATURE OF THE CONNECTION.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Sections (Beveridge’s summary).]]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[1\. The sum of true wisdom—namely, the knowledge of God and of ourselves. Effects of the latter.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[2\. Effects of the knowledge of God, in humbling our pride, unveiling our hypocrisy, showing the absolute perfections of God, and our own utter helplessness.]

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[3\. Effects of the knowledge of God illustrated by examples: 1. of holy patriarchs; 2. of holy angels; 3. of the sun and moon.]

1\. #smallcaps[Our] wisdom, insofar as it ought to be counted true and solid wisdom, consists almost entirely of two parts: the knowledge of God and of ourselves. But since these are bound together by many ties, it is not easy to tell which of the two comes first and gives birth to the other. For, in the first place, no man can look at himself without at once turning his thoughts toward the God in whom he lives and moves (Acts 17:28). For it is perfectly plain that the gifts we possess cannot possibly come from ourselves; indeed, that our very being is nothing else than subsistence in God alone. In the second place, the blessings that fall to us drop by drop from heaven are like streams leading us to the fountain. Here again, the infinite good that dwells in God shows all the more clearly against our poverty. In particular, the miserable ruin into which the revolt of the first man has plunged us compels us to lift our eyes upward: not only so that, hungry and starving, we may ask from there for what we lack, but so that, roused by fear, we may learn humility. For as there is in man something like a world of misery, and ever since we were stripped of the divine attire our shameful nakedness has exposed an immense heap of disgraces, every man must be stung by the consciousness of his own unhappiness, so that he may come at least to some knowledge of God. So our sense of our ignorance, emptiness, poverty, weakness—in short, our depravity and corruption—reminds us#footnote[See Calvin on John 4:10. (Beveridge’s note.)] that in the Lord, and in none but Him, dwell the true light of wisdom, solid strength, the perfect abundance of all good, and the purity of righteousness. And so our own ills spur us to consider the good things of God; indeed, we cannot aspire to Him in earnest until we have begun to be displeased with ourselves. For what man would not gladly rest in himself? Indeed, who does not rest so, as long as he is unknown to himself—that is, as long as he is content with his own gifts, and unaware or forgetful of his misery? So everyone, in coming to know himself, is not only urged to seek God but is also led as by the hand to find him.

2\. On the other hand, it is clear that man never reaches a true knowledge of himself until he has first contemplated the face of God, and then comes down from that sight to look into himself. For (such is our inborn pride) we always seem to ourselves just and upright and wise and holy, until we are convicted, by clear evidence, of our unrighteousness, vileness, folly, and impurity. But we are not convicted if we look only to ourselves and not also to the Lord—He being the only standard by which this judgment can be reached. For since we are all by nature prone to hypocrisy, any empty show of righteousness satisfies us quite well in place of righteousness itself. And since nothing appears within us or around us that is not tainted with very great impurity, then as long as we keep our mind within the bounds of human pollution, anything a little less defiled pleases us as though it were most pure. In the same way an eye that has had nothing but black before it judges an object of an off-white, or even a brownish, color to be perfectly white. Indeed, the bodily senses can give a still clearer picture of how far we are deceived in judging the powers of the mind. If at midday we either look down at the ground or at the things around us that lie open to our view, we think we have very strong and piercing eyesight; but when we look up at the sun and gaze steadily at it, the sight that served excellently on earth is at once so dazzled and confused by its brilliance that we are forced to admit that our sharpness in seeing earthly things is sheer dimness when turned to the sun. So it is, too, in judging our spiritual qualities. As long as we do not look beyond the earth, we are well pleased with our own righteousness, wisdom, and strength; we flatter ourselves in the sweetest terms and think ourselves all but demigods. But if we once begin to raise our thoughts to God, and to reflect what kind of Being he is, and how complete is the perfection of the righteousness and wisdom and strength to which, as a standard, we are bound to be conformed, then what once delighted us with its false show of righteousness will become polluted with the greatest iniquity; what wonderfully deceived us under the name of wisdom will disgust us by its extreme folly; and what wore the look of strength will be condemned as the most miserable impotence. So far are the qualities in us that seem most perfect from matching the divine purity.

3\. Hence the dread and amazement with which, as Scripture everywhere relates, holy men were struck and overwhelmed whenever they beheld the presence of God. For when we see that those who stood firm and confident while God was absent so quake with terror when he shows his glory that the fear of death seizes them—indeed, that they are, in a manner, swallowed up and brought to nothing—we must conclude that men are never rightly touched and moved by a sense of their own lowliness until they have compared themselves with the majesty of God. We have frequent examples of this dismay both in the judges and in the prophets;#footnote[Judg 13:22; Isa 6:5; Ezek 1:28, 3:14.] so much so that it was a common saying among the people of God, “We will die, for we have seen the Lord.” So the Book of Job too, in humbling men with the awareness of their folly, feebleness, and pollution, always draws its chief argument from descriptions of the divine wisdom, strength, and purity.#footnote[Job 9:4, etc. (Beveridge’s note.)] And not without cause: for we see Abraham the more ready to confess himself only dust and ashes the nearer he comes to behold the glory of the Lord (Gen 18:27), and Elijah unable to await His approach with his face uncovered (1 Kgs 19:13); so dreadful is the sight. And what can man do, man who is only rottenness and a worm, when even the Cherubim themselves must veil their faces out of sheer terror (Isa 6:2)? This, undoubtedly, is what the prophet Isaiah means when he says (Isa 24:23), “The moon will be confounded and the sun will be ashamed, when the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts reigns”; that is, when he displays his brightness and brings it nearer, the brightest things will, in comparison, be covered with darkness.

But though the knowledge of God and the knowledge of ourselves are bound together by a mutual tie, the proper order requires that we treat the former first, and then come down to the latter.
